import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/JsBenchResults
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark: what the benchmark is, the results beside the frameworks it
## is compared with (`JsBenchResults`, from site/js-framework-benchmark.json, which `just js-bench
## bench` measures), what they say, the entries, and how to run it (benchmarks/jsframework/).
meta = {
	title: "js-framework-benchmark · Examples",
	description: "Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark, measured beside vanillajs, Solid, Leptos, Joy and React: CPU time, memory, download and first paint, each benchmark's numbers, what they say, and how to run it.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "results", label: "The results" },
		{ id: "each", label: "Each benchmark" },
		{ id: "reading", label: "What they say" },
		{ id: "entries", label: "The entries" },
		{ id: "run", label: "Run it" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<NoHydration>
		<PageHero
			eyebrow="Examples · Benchmarks"
			title="Roc Solid in js-framework-benchmark"
		>
			js-framework-benchmark is the yardstick most web frameworks are measured with: one page, a
			table of rows and six buttons. Its runner opens each framework's page in Chrome, clicks, and
			times how long until the page has painted; then how much memory the page holds, and what it
			downloads. Roc Solid's two entries are in the repository's <code>benchmarks/jsframework/</code>,
			and here they are beside vanillajs (the floor: hand-written DOM code), Solid (the closest
			relative), Leptos (signals in Rust and wasm), Joy (the other Roc framework) and React.
		</PageHero>

		<JsBenchResults />

		<Heading id="reading">What they say</Heading>
		<p>
			The first paint is the best of them: the page is HTML the server rendered (here, written as
			files by <code>rocx build --static</code>), shown before its wasm has loaded. Updating rows that
			are there is close to the others: a row's holes run again only when its data changed (compared
			with <code>==</code>), selecting one patches a class, and a swap moves the two rows that moved.
		</p>
		<p>
			Making rows is where Roc Solid is slower: about two and a half times vanillajs for 1,000 rows,
			and for 10,000. For each row the runtime calls into the wasm about four times (the row and its
			holes), the host notes every allocation each call makes (so a call cut short by a read that
			waits can be freed), Roc writes the row's markup as JSON, and the runtime reads it and makes the
			elements one by one, with comments marking where each row and hole is. Memory follows from the
			same: a computation for the row and each hole, each with what it read. The download is the app's
			wasm and the runtime: half again as much as Joy's, Leptos's or React's, and many times Solid's,
			which is JavaScript alone; the runtime is shipped minified (D-075). The repository's
			<code>TODO.md</code> lists what is left.
		</p>
		<p>
			The first run found worse (D-074): CPU five times the fastest, creating 1,000 rows 217 ms and
			replacing them 798. The host's notes of what runs allocate had filled with a hash map's
			tombstones, so every run cost more than the one before; and a <code>.map</code>'s rows put in
			order moved every row between two swapped ones. Both are fixed, with tests that count rather
			than time.
		</p>

		<Heading id="entries">The entries</Heading>
		<p>
			Each entry is an app of one page whose markup is the vanillajs entry's (the runner finds the
			buttons by id and the rows by class): <code>keyed</code> keeps each row by its id
			(<code>.map</code> with <code>{"key={row.id}"}</code>), so a row moves with its data;
			<code>non-keyed</code> keeps a row by its position (<code>.map_with_index</code> with
			<code>{"key={i}"}</code>), so a swap rewrites the two rows instead. The rows' data is plain
			Roc (<code>Rows.roc</code>): an id, and a label of three words picked by a generator with a
			fixed seed. Nothing is batched or cached beyond what an app would do, and the wasm is built as
			an app ships it.
		</p>
		<p>
			The others are the benchmark's own entries, built as its README says (Leptos's build is in its
			repository). Joy's entries aren't in the benchmark yet: they are built from its 0.35.0 release
			the way Joy's own <code>benchmarks/jsframework/README.md</code> says. Upstream retired its
			non-keyed entries in October 2026; its runner still measures them, so ours, Joy's and
			vanillajs's are kept.
		</p>

		<Heading id="run">Run it</Heading>
		<p>
			The benchmark lives outside the repository, in a clone of krausest/js-framework-benchmark with
			its runner installed, the other entries built and a Chrome for its runner;
			<code>benchmarks/jsframework/README.md</code> says how to set it up, once. Its runner needs a
			small patch (in the same folder): a trace of a click could hold events from before tracing
			began, which it counted as the click's. Then:
		</p>
		<Code source={"just js-bench entries   # Roc Solid's two entries, built into the clone\njust js-bench bench     # them, then two rounds of every entry (about 75 minutes)\njust js-bench bench --rounds=1 --count=3 --benchmarks=01_,05_   # a quick look"} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
		<p>
			The runner is headless, the benchmark's server on port 8089, and each round runs one benchmark at
			a time for every entry, the second round in reverse order. Run it on mains, with nothing else
			heavy running. The results land in <code>site/js-framework-benchmark.json</code>, which this
			page reads: like the <a href={Paths.examples}>examples</a>' numbers, they are measured only when
			asked, and the site's build doesn't measure.
		</p>
	</NoHydration>
